course: medicine
lesson: 46
title: Hurt on the pitch: rolled ankles, dead legs, winded, and when to stop
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the middle of a match your body pumps out adrenaline, a rush chemical that hides pain for a while, so the excited player is the worst judge of how badly they are hurt.
explain: Picture Ollie in Leeds going over on his ankle and hopping about like a pirate: «It's nowt, I'll run it off», he says, and «nowt» is Northern for nothing, but running on a rolled ankle strains the hurt ligaments even more.
explain: A dead leg (Kian in Glasgow yells «Ma leg's deid!», Scots for dead) is a thigh muscle squashed against the bone so tiny vessels bleed inside it; it needs rest and cold, not hard rubbing.
explain: Being winded feels terrifying, but it is the big breathing muscle under the ribs cramping for a moment, and breath usually comes back by itself within a minute or two.
explain: The golden rules: if you can't walk on it properly you're off, if you can't stand you stay down until an adult checks you, and you never hide an injury from the coach.
fact: A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together, so it swells and hurts more as it cools down.
fact: A dead leg is a bruised thigh muscle; cold wrapped in a cloth for about 20 minutes at a time helps, and hard rubbing on a fresh one can make the bleeding inside worse.
fact: Being winded is a short cramp of the diaphragm, the breathing muscle under the ribs; sitting calmly with slow breaths helps, and slapping the back or lifting the arms has no good proof behind it.
fact: Adrenaline hides pain during a game, so the real hurt often shows that evening or the next morning, and an adult, not the excited player, decides whether they play on.
fact: A player who stays down may be badly hurt; the referee can stop play for an injury at any time, and coaches can call for it.
fact: Someone who can't stand should not be dragged or carried off by mates; an adult checks them where they lie and decides how to move them.
fact: A hidden injury played on often gets worse, so a small sprain can turn into weeks out instead of one missed match.
fact: Pain can go before a ligament is strong again, so return to sport goes step by step: walk, jog, run, turns, training, then a match.
fact: Cramp is a muscle tightening on its own, often when it is tired; gentle stretching and rest usually ease it in minutes, and its exact cause is not fully known.
fact: A knee that pops and swells up fast after a twist may have bleeding inside the joint and needs a doctor.
fact: Neck pain with tingling or numb legs after a crash means the person must be kept still and an adult calls 999 in the UK or 911 in the US.
fact: Touchline talk: «grand» and «sound» mean fine in Ireland, «eejit» is an Irish word for idiot, «arvo» is afternoon in Australia, «chur» is New Zealand for thanks or yes, «lekker» is South African for great, and «now now» there means soon.
exercise: What does a rolled ankle actually hurt? | The ligaments that hold the joint together | The skin and the thin bones on top of the foot | The bone marrow inside the shin | The nerves in the big toe only
exercise: Why does a rolled ankle often hurt more an hour later? | It swells and stiffens as it cools down | The bone starts to grow back crooked under the skin | The pain was only imagined during the game | Air gets trapped inside the joint and presses on it
exercise: What is a dead leg? | A thigh muscle squashed so tiny vessels bleed inside | A leg that has stopped getting any blood | A bone that has cracked right through | A nerve that has been cut in half
exercise: What helps a fresh dead leg? | Rest and something cold wrapped in a cloth | Hard rubbing with your knuckles until it feels warm again | Running on it to loosen it up | A hot bath straight away
exercise: What is happening when you are winded? | The breathing muscle under the ribs cramps briefly | Your lungs have burst from the hit | Your heart has stopped for a moment | Your stomach has turned upside down
exercise: How long does breath usually take to come back after being winded? | About a minute or two, by itself | About an hour of lying flat | Only after someone slaps your back | Never, without a doctor's machine
exercise: Why is an excited player the worst judge of their own injury? | Adrenaline hides pain during the game | Players always lie about everything | Pain only exists after the final whistle | Children cannot feel pain in matches
exercise: A player stays down and the other team keeps dribbling. Who can stop the game? | The referee, and a coach can call for it | Nobody can stop it until the ball goes out of play | Only the hurt player's mum or dad | Only the captain of the other team
exercise: Your teammate can't stand. What should happen? | He stays down and an adult checks him there | Two mates drag him off the pitch quickly by his arms | He gets up quickly to save time | Someone carries him on their back
exercise: Why tell the coach about a hurt ankle before a big final? | A hidden injury played on often gets much worse | The coach likes to collect stories about injuries | Telling the coach makes the pain go away at once | Coaches give out medals for being honest
exercise: Your ankle stopped hurting after a week. What next? | Build back step by step before a full match | Play a full match the same afternoon | Never play sport again | Run as fast as you can to test it
exercise: Your calf suddenly locks hard near the end of a game. What helps most? | Stop, stretch it gently and rest | Keep sprinting hard until the muscle lets go | Eat a banana quickly and then play on | Punch the muscle a few times to loosen it
exercise: A knee pops in a tackle and swells like a melon within the hour. What does it need? | An adult and a doctor to check it | Just more rugby to loosen it | A tight bandage and a sprint | Nothing, swelling is always a bruise
exercise: After a crash a player says his neck hurts and his legs tingle. What should happen? | Keep him still while an adult calls 999 | Sit him up and give him a drink of cold water | Help him walk to the bench slowly, one step at a time | Pull his head gently to stretch it
exercise: In Ireland a player says «I'm grand, sound». What does he mean? | That he's fine | That he's hurt badly | That he's very hungry | That he's going home
exercise: Who decides if a hurt child plays on? | An adult such as the coach or a parent | The hurt child, because they know best | The loudest parent on the touchline | The other team's captain
test: ollie: «its nowt, ill run it off» — he can walk 1 step out of 4. what now? | He comes off; you don't run off a hurt ankle | He runs it off, that's what «nowt» means in Yorkshire | He hops on the other foot and plays on | He sprints to warm the ankle up
test: A dad yells «Play on, he's faking!» while a lad stays down. Who's right? | The coach calling for a stop; the lad may be badly hurt | The dad; kids who stay down on the grass are always faking it | The dad, as long as the ball's in play | Nobody; the lad should crawl off himself
test: Kian's mate: «Ah'll fix yer deid leg, wee man», rubbing it hard. Good shout? | No, hard rubbing can make the bleeding inside worse | Yes, rubbing hard drains the blood out | Yes, the harder the better | No, he should slap it instead
test: A netball hits Aisha in the tummy and she gasps «I can't breathe!». Her friend lifts her arms and slaps her back. Better plan? | Sit her down calmly and help her take slow breaths | Slap her back harder until the air goes back in | Lay her flat and press hard on her tummy | Make her run a lap of the court to open her lungs up
test: Liam in Cork: «I'm grand, put me back on!» His mam says no. Why is she right? | Pain often shows later, once the excitement wears off | Mams are always right about football | Being grand is against the rules | He was playing too well and the rest of the team needed a go
test: «C'mon bud, walk it off, eh» — two mates drag Mateo by his arms. What's wrong? | Dragging can twist the injury; he should wait for an adult | Nothing, dragging is the fastest first aid | They should drag him off by the legs instead, it's gentler | They should leave him there for the whole game
test: Zara: «don't snitch bruv, coach will drop me». She plays the final on a bad ankle. Most likely result? | The sprain gets worse and she's out for weeks | The ankle heals faster from all the exercise in the final | Nothing, finals don't cause injuries | She plays better because she's angry
test: Noah: «no pain = healed, sweet as, full game this arvo». Right? | No, the ligament can still be weak; build up step by step | Yes, no pain always means healed | Yes, and he should play two games | No, he should wait two whole years
test: «Bananas cure cramp, get me one quick!» What's true? | Cramp's cause isn't fully known; gentle stretching and rest help | A banana fixes cramp in ten seconds | Cramp only happens to people who hate bananas | Salt in your boots stops cramp
test: Siya, an hour after a pop in his knee: «I'm fine now now, bru». The knee's like a melon. What now? | An adult gets it checked by a doctor | Back on for the second half, it's only swelling | A hot bath and a hard massage tonight | Wait a month and see if it goes down on its own
test: A parent runs on to sit up a lad who says his neck hurts and his legs tingle. What would happen if he's moved? | A neck injury could be made much worse | Moving him would fix the tingling in his legs | Nothing, necks can't really be hurt in sport like that | He'd feel better from sitting up and the water
test: What would happen if every hurt player just «ran it off»? | Small injuries would turn into long ones | Everyone would get fitter and stronger from playing on | Injuries would heal twice as fast | Nobody would ever need a physio
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an injured participant ought not to be the arbiter of his own fitness to continue. | Adrenaline released during play may conceal the severity of pain | Participants are prohibited from expressing any opinion upon the matter | Pain cannot be perceived until the conclusion of play | Injuries sustained in play are invariably trivial
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the condition colloquially termed «winded». | A transient spasm of the diaphragm which ordinarily resolves within minutes | A rupture of the lungs requiring immediate surgery | A cessation of the heart lasting several hours | A condition remedied solely by striking the back
exam: It is given that a participant reports pain in the neck accompanied by tingling in the legs following a collision. State the appropriate course of action. | The participant is to be kept still whilst an adult summons emergency services | The participant is to be raised to a seated posture and given water at once, so as to aid recovery | The participant is to be assisted to walk from the field | The participant is to resume play once the tingling subsides
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recommended return to sport following a sprained ankle? | A graduated progression from walking through training to competition | An immediate return upon cessation of pain | A permanent withdrawal from all physical activity | A return to full competition on the same day
